l.a:
Teacher models and guides pupils to complete activities in the workbook:
Pupils read the words and sort them into correct long /a/, long /i/ or long /o/ boxes. (e.g. make, cake, name, rose, note, cone, time, line, side)
With picture support, pupils write the missing letters and then read the words with targeted sounds. (e.g: c__k__ → cake; b__n__ → bone; t__m__ → time; r__s__ → rose; f__v__ → five;
b__k__ → bike);
Pupils rhyme words that have the same end sounds; look again at the words in the box they have sorted above and write the words that rhyme. (e.g. fine →line; hide →five; nose →rose).

Teacher models and guides pupils to complete activities in the workbook:
Pupils write the letter U u. With picture support, pupils name the images of objects with short /u/: (e.g. umbrella, up);
The teacher introduces long /u/ and explains the role of silent –e in making long u sound.
With picture support, pupils read long /u/ words: (e.g. tube, flute, cube, mule, june);
Pupils read the words and then sort them into correct short /u/ or long /u/ boxes. (e.g. tub, us, rug, hug, nut; fuse, flute, use, mute, cute);
Pupils look at the words in the boxes they just made and write words that rhyme. (e.g. mute →cute; ruse →fuse; flute →cute).

l.c:
Key words:
Short /e/: pet, net, bed, pen, let, red, hen, ten, get, pen Long /e/: Pete, these, theme, eve;
/-ee/ (long e): see, bee, tree, free, feet, meet, seed, week, keep, deep, tree, teeth, sheep, wheel, knee

l.a:
Teacher models and guides pupils to complete activities in the workbook:
Pupils write the letter E e. Pupils read the short /e/ words with short /e/: (e.g. net, bed, red, pen, let, hen);
The teacher introduces long /e/ with picture support. Pupils read the long /e/ words, and the teacher explains the role of silent -e in making long /e/ sound: (e.g. these, here, Pete, feet, see, bee, tree, teeth, sheep, wheel, knee);
With picture support, pupils write the missing letters and read the words with targeted sounds. (e.g: tr__ → tree; b__ → bee; sh___p → sheep; f___t → feet; wh__l → wheel);

Pupils practice long /e/ by reading and writing long /e/ words. (e.g. Pete, free);
Pupils practice long /e/ by reading and matching the word with long /e/ with the picture. (e.g. see, bee, feet, teeth);
Teacher guides pupils to read the weekly decodable text and to talk and write about it:
Before reading: Pupils read and copy sight words (there, says, of, are). The teacher shows the picture and reads the title with pupils. Pupils make predictions based on the title and the pictures. During reading: Pupils whisper read the decodable text: Steve and the sheep.
The teacher models how to read as pupils follow along tracing with fingers. Pupils choral-read louder with the teacher. Pupils read one by one, in pairs, bench by bench, row by row, etc.

Teacher models and guides pupils to complete activities in the workbook:
With picture support, pupils read and spell words with /k/ sound. The teacher explains different patterns in which the sound /k/ is used. (e.g. the sound /k/ is used (1) with short vowels, as -ck in: back, sock, duck, truck/0; (2) with long vowels, as -ke in bike, snake, lake, rake);
With picture support, pupils write the missing letters and read the words with targeted sounds. (e.g: du__ → duck; bi___ → bike; br___ → brick; la___ → lake; so___→ sock)
Pupils read the words and then sort them into correct /k/ sound box (as -ck or -ke); (e.g. rock, sack, lick, pick and brick go to -ck box; take, bake, like, joke and snake go to -ke box);

Teacher models and guides pupils to complete activities in the workbook:
Pupils read these words. (e.g. Long /u/: use, rule, tube, cute, rude; Long /o/: hope, rope, note, woke, cone, stone);
Read the words, then sort/ write each word in the correct box. (e.g. Long /u/: cube, cute, mute;
Long /o/: poke, bone, note);
With picture support, pupils write the missing letters and read the words with targeted sounds. (e.g: t__b__ → tube; r__p__ → rope; c__b__ → cube; fl__t__ → flute; n__s__→ nose);
Pupils look at the words in the boxes they just made and write words that rhyme. (e.g. cute →cute; hope → rope tube →cube).

l.c:
Review words:
use, rule, tube, cute, rude, hope, rope, note, woke, cone, stone;
Sight words: her, she and think; Decodable text: Duke is lost

Practice words:
date, fail, say, day, rain, pail, hey;
Sight words: want, after and we; Decodable text: The rainy day

l.o:
Read and spell short words with long
/u/patterns (-oa- and
-ow); Identify long /u/ sounds in word through pictures;

l.a:
With picture support, pupils write the missing letters and read the words with long /o/ sound. (e.g: b__t → boat;
g__t → goat; s__p → soap; r__d → road; c__t → coat; wind_→ window);
Teacher guides pupils to read the weekly decodable text and to talk and write about it:
Before reading: Pupils read and copy sight words (off, out, look, eating). The teacher shows the picture and reads the title with pupils. Pupils make predictions based on the title and the pictures.
During reading: Pupils whisper read the decodable text: A goat on a boat.
The teacher models how to read as pupils follow along tracing with fingers. Pupils choral-read louder with the teacher. Pupils read one by one, in pairs, bench by bench, row by row, etc. After reading: Check pupils’ predictions. Ask comprehension questions and guide pupils to know how and where they can find the answers in the story. (e.g: Who is on the boat? What does the goat eat? What happened to the goat and the boy?).

Teacher models and guides pupils to complete activities in the workbook:
Pupils practice segmenting, blending and reading words with long /o/ as -oa-, -ow and -o_e. E.g.
-oa-: boat, toad, soap, coat, road, roam, load, goat, float;
-ow: low, snow, row, know, pillow, yellow, blow, show, grow;
-o_e: pole, hope, bone, home, vote).
Pupils read the words, then sort/ write each word in the correct box. (e.g. -oa-: coat, road / -ow: pillow, grow /
-o_e: pole, bone);
Pupils look at the words in the boxes they just made and write words that rhyme. (e.g. Toad→ road →coat; glow →grow →blow).
With pictures pupils fill the missing letters and read the words out loud.

Oral quiz: In pairs pupils tell the time, give dates in days, months, years and state the number of minutes/ hours/ months;
Written exercise: Pupils write the date and complete the sentence:
● We celebrate:
- Heroes’ Day on………………;
- Liberation Day on…………;
- Christmas Day on………….;

Teacher models and guides pupils to complete activities in the workbook:
Pupils practice segmenting, blending and reading long /o/ and long /a/ words: (e.g.
-ow: blow, grow, snow, show, pillow, yellow;
-oa: goat, toad, goal, road, soap;
-o_e: pole, note, cone, home;
-a_e: late, lake, date, take, name;
-ay: day, play, tray, clay, gray;
-ai: pain, main, gain, rain, mail, tail, sail);
Read the words, then sort/ write each word in the correct box. (e.g. -oa--: road, load, toad /-ow: row, show, pillow, blow; grow / -o_e: rope, pole, home, cone);
Pupils look at the words in the boxes they just made above and write words that rhyme. (e.g.
road→ load →toad →boat; pillow→ blow → grow; cone →bone → home).

Teacher models and guides pupils to complete activities in the workbook:
With picture support, pupils read words with long /i/ patterns. e.g.
-i_e: time, bite, ride, bike, five;
-ie: pie, lie, tie, die;
-igh: right, sight, tight, light, night, fight
Read the words, then sort/ write each word in the correct box. e.g.
-i_e-: five, time, ride, bike, dive;
-igh: light, right, high, night;
-ie: tie, pie, die, lie
Pupils look at the words in the boxes they just made above and write words that rhyme. e.g.
bright → night →light →right;
lie → tie →pie →die

Teacher models and guides pupils to complete activities in the workbook:
With picture support; pupils read the -ee- words. (e.g. feet see, bee, tree, teeth, sheep, wheel);
The teacher writes -ee- words on the board and explains that long /e/ can be made with -ee-;
Pupils write -ee- to make each word. Then read the words. (e.g. feet, see, bee, teeth, tree, sheep);
With picture support; pupils read the -ea- words. (e.g. tea, read, meat, eat, seat);
The teacher writes -ea- words on the board and explains that long /e/ can be made with -ea-;
Pupils write -ea- to make each word. Then read the words. (e.g. read, meat, eat, seat, tea);
Pupils read the words and write/ sort each word in the correct -ea- or -ee- box.
(e.g. sea, seat, tea and beat go to -ea- box;
tree, need, seed and keep go to -ee- box);
With picture support, pupils complete the sentences by writing the missing words; and then read the sentences. (e.g. Eve takes a seat. We read a good book;
I brush my teeth. Then I seat and drink tea. I see a bee. It is in the tree).

Oral quiz: In pairs pupils report past events, tell each other stories and describe future plans;
Reading exercise: Pupils read words or short sentences with targeted sounds from weeks 12 and 13 of term 1 and week 1 of term 2. (e.g: blow, snow, road, take, date, note, pain, gain, time, five, lie, night, feet, wheel, read, seat); Written exercise: Pupils complete the following activities: Filling in missing letters in phonics words and reading the words made. (e.g. pl___ne → plane, s___t → seat; t___ → tea, n_n_ → nine, l___t → light);
Dictation of decodable words with sounds previously learned (e.g: seat, rain, right, dive).

Teacher models and guides pupils to complete activities in the workbook:
With picture support; pupils read the -oo- words. (e.g. moon, pool, root, noon, food, spoon);
Pupils write the missing -oo- to complete each word. Then read the words. (e.g. m___n → moon; p____l → pool; r____t → root; n____n → noon;
f____d → food; sp____n → spoon);
With picture support; pupils read the -ew- words. (e.g. grew, screw, stew, flew);
The teacher writes -ew- words on the board and explains that long /u/ can be made with -ew-;
Pupils write -ew- to complete each word. Then read the words. (e.g. grew, screw, stew, flew);
Pupils read the words and write/ sort each word in the correct -u_e-, -ew- or -ee- box.
(e.g. dew, flew and grew go to -ew- box;
noon, food and cool go to -oo- box;
dune, huge and cube go to -u_e- box.
With picture support, pupils complete the sentences by writing the missing words; and then read the sentences. (e.g. It is a new moon. Bats flew in the night;
At noon, I eat food. I use my spoon; It is noon. Luke swims in a pool).

l.o:
Read a decodable text with words containing -ew,
-oo, and u_e patterns;
Talk and write about the text.

l.a:
Teacher models and guides pupils to complete activities in the workbook:
With picture support, pupils write the missing words with targeted sounds to complete the sentence and then read the sentences. (e.g.
Pete plants a seed. It has roots;
It grew big. Now, it is a tree;
At 6 o’clock, I drink tea with a spoon;
At noon, we eat. I like stew).
Pupils read the long /i/ words. Then write each word/ sort the words in the i_e or -igh- correct box. (e.g. kite, ride, fine and time go to i_e box; night, right, high and light go to -igh- box);
Pupils read the long /u/ words. Then write each word/ sort the words in the -u_e-, -ew or -oo- correct box. (e.g. cute, tube, tune and mule go to -u_e- box;
few, grew, new and chew go to -ew box;
noon, spoon, food and soon go to -oo- box).

l.c:
Review words: Sheep, read, tie, moon, bike, screw, flute
Sight words: touch, head, eyes and write;
Decodable text: A poem about my body;

l.a:
Teacher models and guides pupils to complete activities in the workbook:
With picture support, pupils read the -or- words. (e.g. horn, corn, horse, fork, torch, forest, north, orange, score, store);
Pupils write -or- to make each word, then read the words. (e.g. h__n → horn; c__n → corn; h__se → horse; f___k → fork; t__ch → torch; f__est → forest;
n__th → north; __ange → orange; sc__e → score; st__e → store);
Pupils read the -or- words and sort each word in the correct box. (e.g. for, fork, horn and corn go to -or box; store, more, shore and wore go to -ore box);
With picture support, pupils write the missing words with targeted sounds to complete the sentence and then read the sentences. (e.g.
I go to the forest. I use my torch to see;
I get corn at the store. I feed it to my horse;
I run north. I score a goal);

l.c:
Practice words: Horn, fork, corn, torch, forest
Sight words: here, they, called and give;
Decodable text: Flora on the farm;

l.a:
Teacher models and guides pupils to complete activities in the workbook:
With picture support, pupils read the -ar- words. (e.g. car, jar, star, farm, March, marker, market, garden); Pupils write -ar- to make each word, then read the words. (e.g. c__ → car; j__ → jar; st__ → star; f___m → farm; m__ker → marker; m__ket → market; g___den → garden; M__ch → March);
Pupils read the -ar- words and sort each word in the correct box. (e.g. car, bar, far and cart go to -ar box; horn, fork, corn and born go to -or box);
With picture support, pupils write the missing words with targeted sounds (-ar and -or) to complete the sentence and then read the sentences.
(e.g. I go to the farm. I go by car;
In the dark, I see a star. It is far;
I picked an orange. I make jam;
I put it in a jar.)

l.c:
Practice words: car, March, jar, marker, star
Sight words: every, break, parents, and some;
Decodable text: What do you do every day?

Key words:
-are: care, fare, bare, dare, mare, rare, share, stare, scare;
-air: air, fair, hair, pair, chair, stair;

l.a:
Teacher models and guides pupils to complete activities in the workbook:
With picture support, pupils read the -air- words. (e.g. chair, stairs, air, pair, hair);
Pupils write -air- to make each word, then read the words. (e.g. ch__ → chair; st__s → stairs; p__ → pair; h___ → hair);
The teacher explains to pupils that the sound /air/ can be spelled -air and -are-;
With picture support, pupils read the -are- words. (e.g. share, care, stare, scare);
Pupils write -are- to make each word, then read the words. (e.g. sh__ → share; st__ → stare; c__ → care; sc___ → scare);
Pupils read the -air- and -are words and sort each word in the correct box. (e.g. fair, air, chair, pair, care, dare, bare, rare);
With picture support, pupils write the missing words with targeted sounds (-air and -are) to complete the sentence and then read the sentences. (e.g. I have a new pair of shoes. I go up the stairs. I sit on the chair. I brush my hair. The girl stares at my car. I share with her).

Teacher models and guides pupils to complete activities in the workbook:
With picture support, pupils read the -ir words. (e.g. bird, girl, shirt, circle, dirty, skirt, thirty, thirteen);
Pupils write the missing -ir- to make the word. Then read the words. (e.g. b____d → bird; g____l → girl; sh___t → shirt; c___cle → circle; d___ty → dirty; sk___t → skirt;
th__ty → thirty; th__teen → thirteen); Pupils read the words and write/ sort each word in the correct box. (e.g. sir, dirty, third, stir, car, far, dark, bar);
With picture support, pupils write the missing words with targeted /ir/ sound to complete the sentence and then read the sentences. (e.g. The girls fall. Her skirt is dirty;
Look at the bird! They are thirty. They fly in a circle. Mama stirred the pot. She gets food on her shirt.

l.c:
Review key words:
-ir: sir, fir, stir, bird, third, girl, skirt, shirt, dirty, firm, thirteen;
-er: her, fern, term, perk, paper, water, flower, mother, hammer;
-ur: fur, burn, curl, purse, hurt, turtle

l.a:
Teacher models and guides pupils to complete activities in the workbook:
With picture support, pupils read the sounds in the word then read the word. (e.g. out, ouch, shout, cloud, thousand, count, mouth, South);
Pupils write the missing -ou- to make the word. Then read the words. (e.g. ____t → out; ____t → ouch; sh___t → shout; cl___ d→ cloud; c__nt → count; s___th → south; m___th → mouth);
With picture support, pupils write the missing words with targeted /ou/ sound to complete the sentence and then read the sentences. (e.g. I want to go out. There is no cloud. I will shout and play. Pat wants to count. She will use her mouth to count to a thousand. There is a cloud. It may rain. I run to the south. I fall. “ouch”, I say.)

Teacher models and guides pupils to complete activities in the workbook:
With picture support, pupils read the sounds in the word then read the word. (e.g. cow, down, frown, now, gown, bow, how, plow);
The teacher explains to pupils that -ow- makes the same sound as -ou-;
Pupils write the missing -ow- to make the word. Then read the words. (e.g. d___n → down; c____ → cow; n____ → now; fr___n→ frown; g___n → gown; h___ → how; b___ → bow; pl___ → plow);
Pupils read the words and the write/ sort each word in the correct box. (e.g. out, shout, loud, mouth and ouch go to -ou- box;
cow, now, down, how and wow go to -ow- box); With picture support, pupils write the missing words with targeted /ow/ sound to complete the sentence and then read the sentences. (e.g.
The cow is big. Father will use it to plow the farm. IIt is now time to dance. The woman is wearing a gown. I dance and do not fall down. I bow).

Teacher models and guides pupils to complete activities in the workbook:
With picture support, pupils read the sounds in the word then read the word. (e.g. boil, coin, oil, join, soil, spoil, point, toilet);
Pupils write the missing -oi- to make the word. Then read the words. (e.g. b___l → boil; ____l → oil; c____n → coin; j___n→ join; s___l → soil; p___nt→ point; sp___l → spoil); With picture support, pupils write the missing words with targeted /oi/ sound to complete the sentence and then read the sentences. (e.g. We point to the soil. There is a coin! I boil a pot of water. Then I get the oil. At school, I go to the toilet. I come back and join my friends).

Teacher models and guides pupils to complete activities in the workbook:
With picture support, pupils read the sounds in the word then read the word. (e.g. boy, toys, joy, soy, enjoy, destroy, convoy, cowboy);
The teacher explains to pupils that -oy makes sound /oi/; Pupils write the missing -oy- to make the word. Then read the words. (e.g. b___ → boy; t____s → toys; j____ → joy; s____→ soy; enj___ → enjoy);
Pupils read the words and the write/ sort each word in the correct box. (e.g. joy, ploy, toy, boy and enjoy go to -oy- box; oil, spoil, boil, soil and join go to -oi- box);
Pupils look at the table they have just made and write words that rhyme. (e.g. joy →toy →boy; boil → soil →oil);
With picture support, pupils write the missing words with targeted /oy/ sound to complete the sentence and then read the sentences. (e.g. The girl and the boy play with the toys. They have so much joy; Troy puts the water on the fire to boil. Sam will join him for tea; The oil is spoil. I will put it on the soil.

Teacher models and guides pupils to complete activities in the workbook:
With picture support, pupils write the missing words with /ou/, /ow/, /oi/ and /oy/ sounds to complete the sentence and then read the sentences. (e.g. Roy plays with his toy. Then he falls down. “ouch!” he shouted; Joy wants to go out. She wants to see a cow. She asks Roy to join her.)
Pupils read the words and the write/ sort each word in the correct box. (e.g. found, loud, sound and round go to -ou- box; bow, how, now and cow go to -ow- box);
Pupils look at the table they have just made and write words that rhyme. (e.g. bow →how →now; round → found →sound);
Pupils read the -oi- words and write/ sort each word in the correct box. (e.g. joy, enjoy, soy and toy go to -oy- box;
boil, coil, soil and spoil go to -oi- box);
Pupils look at the table they have just made and write words that rhyme. (e.g. joy →toy →soy; coil → boil→ soil);

Teacher models and guides pupils to complete activities in the workbook:
With picture support, pupils read the sounds in the word then read the word. (e.g. yawn, shawl, claws, crawl, saw, draw, paw, straw);
Pupils write the missing -aw- to make the word. Then read the words. (e.g. y___n → yawn; dr____ → draw; cr____l → crawl; str____ → straw; s___ → saw; sh___l → shawl; p___ → paw; cl___s → claws);
With picture support, pupils write the missing words with targeted sound to complete the sentence and then read the sentences. (e.g. The baby plays with the toys. The baby wants to sleep. The baby yawns! I want to draw. I will draw the saw. Then I will drink milk with a straw. The cat will catch the rat. It will use its paw. The paw has long claws.

Teacher models and guides pupils to complete activities in the workbook:
With picture support, pupils read the sound in the word then read the words. (e.g. August, aunt, laundry, astronaut, applause, author, launch, sauce);
The teacher explains to pupils that /au/ makes the same sound as /aw/;
Pupils write the missing -au- to make the word. Then read the words. (e.g.
s___ce → sauce; ____ nt → aunt;
l____nch → launch; l____ ndry→ laundry); Pupils read the words and write/ sort each word in the correct box. (E.g. straw, saw, shawl and jaw go to -aw- box; aunt, cause, sauce and pause go to -au- box);
Pupils look at the table they have just made and write words that rhyme. (e.g. jaw →saw →paw; crawl →shawl →claw);
With picture support, pupils write the missing words with targeted sound to complete the sentence and then read the sentences. (e.g.
This is my aunt. At night, she wears a shawl; The spaceship has a big launch. here is applause for the astronaut;
Laura spills some sauce on her shawl.

Teacher models and guides pupils to complete activities in the workbook:
With picture support, pupils read the sound in the word then read the words. (e.g. walk, chalk, stalk, talk)
The teacher explains to pupils that -alk has a silent letter l;
Pupils write the missing -alk to make the word. Then read the words. (e.g. w___ → walk; t____ → talk; ch____ → chalk; st____ → stalk);
With picture support, pupils read the sound in the word then read the words. (e.g. tall, wall, call, hall, fall)
The teacher explains to pupils that -all makes the sound /ol/
Pupils write the missing -all to make the word. Then read the words. (e.g. h___ → hall; c____ → call; f____ → fall; w____ → wall; t_____ → tall); Pupils read the words and write/ sort each word in the correct box. (E.g. tall, ball, call and wall go to -all- box; talk, stalk, walk and chalk go to -alk box);
With picture support, pupils write the missing words with targeted sound to complete the sentence and then read the sentences. (e.g.
The boy goes for a walk and then he falls;
My brother is tall. He can see the top of the wall; My mother is on a call. She wants to talk).

Teacher models and guides pupils to complete activities in the workbook:
With picture support, pupils write the missing words with /aw/, /au/, /alk/ and /all/ sounds to complete the sentence and then read the sentences. (e.g.
The baby wants to crawl. The baby falls. I call my mother;
My father is tall. He can get the ball on top of the wall); Pupils read the words and the write/ sort each word in the correct box. (e.g. paw, law, jaw and claw go to -aw- box;
talk, walk, chalk and stalk go to -alk- box);
Pupils look at the table they have just made and write words that rhyme. (e.g. paw →jaw →law →claw; walk → talk →stalk → chalk);

